          {
            "topic": "rsc.ts vs server.ts",
            "choice": "One `server.ts` service taking an explicit session context; no rsc.ts.",
            "rationale": "The page and the GET handler need the identical read, so a current-request rsc.ts would be a one-to-one facade over server.ts. The session object carries actor and cookie client together, satisfying explicit identity without letting server.ts rediscover the user."
          },
          {
            "topic": "query-cache.ts",
            "choice": "Not created.",
            "rationale": "It is admitted only when the same serializable query-key identity is consumed by both a server prefetch/hydration path and a browser path. The island is seeded through initialData props from the RSC render, so the key exists only in client/."
          },
          {
            "topic": "ui/ segment",
            "choice": "Panel and both forms stay route-private under app/(app)/labels/_components/.",
            "rationale": "Neither is reused nor part of the capability's public contract; promotion would create a segment with no consumer."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Browser read transport",
            "choice": "GET /api/labels Route Handler with private ETag revalidation, not a Server Action.",
            "rationale": "Browser-owned reads use GET handlers or streams; Server Actions are UI command boundaries. The task also requires cacheable HTTP GETs, which only a Route Handler can own (status, ETag, Cache-Control, Vary)."
